Read results back off the GPU on macOS 12 and 13.

The bug: llama.cpp asks Metal to wrap the buffer it wants results written into,
with `newBufferWithBytesNoCopy:`. That call wants a page-aligned address and
gets a 32-byte-aligned one, because the buffer comes from the CPU backend.
macOS 14 and later take it anyway. macOS 12 and 13 return nil, upstream asserts
on it, and `llama-server` aborts on its first decode:

    ggml/src/ggml-metal/ggml-metal-context.m:361: GGML_ASSERT(buf_dst) failed

It is the readback path, not the model. Nothing about the flags, the context
size, the load mode or the number of GPU layers changes it, and `-ngl 1` aborts
exactly as `-ngl 999` does. The only thing that avoids it is keeping the model
off the GPU altogether, which costs a factor of twelve.

The fix: when Metal refuses the pointer, drain the queue and copy the result out
of the source buffer by hand. Buffers here are MTLResourceStorageModeShared and
Apple silicon has one memory pool, so the bytes are already somewhere the CPU
can read. It costs the asynchronous write-back, and only on the machines that
would otherwise die. macOS 14 and later never reach it — `buf_dst` is not nil
there — so nothing changes for anyone who is working today.

Which is also the problem with testing it, so `GGML_METAL_FORCE_READBACK=1`
takes the slow path on any Mac. Six releases shipped a theory about this crash
that could not be run anywhere; the fix should not be another one.

Upstream: https://github.com/ggml-org/llama.cpp/issues/16266 — reported Sept
2025, closed by a stale bot in Dec 2025 with nothing fixed. The one attempt at a
fix (#18738) was withdrawn: the maintainer has no machine old enough to
reproduce on and would rather find why the pointer is unaligned than guard the
call. Until that happens this patch is ours to carry.

Against: llama.cpp tag b10715.

--- a/ggml/src/ggml-metal/ggml-metal-context.m
+++ b/ggml/src/ggml-metal/ggml-metal-context.m
@@ -353,13 +353,23 @@
 void ggml_metal_get_tensor_async(ggml_metal_t ctx, const struct ggml_tensor * tensor, void * data, size_t offset, size_t size) {
     @autoreleasepool {
         id<MTLDevice> device = ggml_metal_device_get_obj(ctx->dev);
-        id<MTLBuffer> buf_dst = [device newBufferWithBytesNoCopy:data
-                                                          length:size
-                                                         options:MTLResourceStorageModeShared
-                                                     deallocator:nil];
 
-        GGML_ASSERT(buf_dst);
+        // Take the slow path even where the fast one would work. There is no
+        // way to make a current macOS refuse a pointer, so without this the
+        // fallback below could only ever be exercised on the machines that were
+        // already broken — which is how it came to be broken for six releases.
+        static int force_readback = -1;
+        if (force_readback < 0) {
+            const char * env = getenv("GGML_METAL_FORCE_READBACK");
+            force_readback = env != NULL && env[0] != '\0' && env[0] != '0';
+        }
 
+        id<MTLBuffer> buf_dst = force_readback ? nil :
+            [device newBufferWithBytesNoCopy:data
+                                      length:size
+                                     options:MTLResourceStorageModeShared
+                                 deallocator:nil];
+
         struct ggml_metal_buffer_id bid_src = ggml_metal_get_buffer_id(tensor);
         if (bid_src.metal == nil) {
             GGML_ABORT("%s: failed to find buffer for tensor '%s'\n", __func__, tensor->name);
@@ -367,6 +377,31 @@
 
         bid_src.offs += offset;
 
+        // On macOS 12 and 13, newBufferWithBytesNoCopy: returns nil instead of
+        // wrapping the pointer: it wants a page-aligned address, and `data` is
+        // llama.cpp's logits buffer, which the CPU backend aligns to 32 bytes.
+        // macOS 14 and later accept the same pointer, which is why this goes
+        // unseen on a current Mac. Upstream asserts, so every decode on an
+        // older one kills the process.
+        //
+        // Nothing is wrong with the GPU, and the result is already in memory
+        // both sides can read: buffers here are MTLResourceStorageModeShared,
+        // and Apple silicon has one pool. So drain the queue and copy straight
+        // out of the source buffer. This gives up the asynchronous write-back,
+        // which is why it is reached only when the fast path has refused.
+        if (buf_dst == nil) {
+            ggml_metal_synchronize(ctx);
+
+            const char * src = (const char *) [(id<MTLBuffer>) bid_src.metal contents];
+            if (src == NULL) {
+                GGML_ABORT("%s: cannot read tensor '%s' back from the GPU\n", __func__, tensor->name);
+            }
+
+            memcpy(data, src + bid_src.offs, size);
+
+            return;
+        }
+
         // queue the copy operation into the queue of the Metal context
         // this will be queued at the end, after any currently ongoing GPU operations
         id<MTLCommandQueue> queue = ggml_metal_device_get_queue(ctx->dev);
